<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finding-eigen" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Finding Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

   <p>
    So far we have verified whether a given vector was an eigenvector of a matrix (and from that we could see the
    eigenvalue). But without already being given the eigenvector, we still have a way of finding the eigenvalues!
    Again, we will borrow some facts from linear algebra, and won't have time to fully justify all of it.
  </p>



  <fact xml:id="fact-eigenvalue-determinant">
    <title>Computing Eigenvalues</title>
    <p>
      <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> exactly when <m>\lambda</m> satisfies:
      <me>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0.</me>
    </p>
  </fact>

  <proof> <title> Justification on why solving <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0 </m> finds the eigenvalues (can skim) </title>
      <p>
    Notice that
    <m>A\mathbf{v} = \lambda\mathbf{v}</m> is equivalent to:
    <me>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}.</me>
    So <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue exactly when this equation has a <em>non-zero</em>
    solution <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.</p>

    <p>  A standard result from linear algebra says this happens
    exactly when the determinant of the matrix <m>A - \lambda I</m> is zero.
  </p>
     </proof>

  <p>
    To find <m>A - \lambda I</m>, we note that subtracting <m>\lambda I</m> just subtracts <m>\lambda</m>
    along the main diagonal of <m>A</m>:
    <md>
      <mrow>A - \lambda I
        \amp= \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}
          - \lambda \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}
          - \begin{bmatrix} \lambda \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp \lambda \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \begin{bmatrix} a - \lambda \amp b \\ c \amp d - \lambda \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
    </md>
    So the steps for finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors are:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Subtract <m>\lambda</m> along the diagonal to compute <m>A - \lambda I</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
           Take the determinant of <m>A - \lambda I</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Set the determinant equal to <m>0</m>, and solve for <m>\lambda</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Last, find the eigenvectors
    that go with each eigenvalue.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: FINDING THE EIGENVALUES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

<p>First we do an example with steps 1-3 to find the eigenvalues of a matrix. </p>
  <example xml:id="ex-finding-eigenvalues">
    <title>Finding the Eigenvalues</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the eigenvalues of
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We will break this up into two steps. Step 1 is to compute <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m>. Step 2
        is to set it equal to <m>0</m> and solve for <m>\lambda</m>.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Step 1: Computing <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m> </title>
      <p>
        Subtracting <m>\lambda</m> along
        the diagonal and computing the determinant:
        <md>
          <mrow>\det(A - \lambda I)
            \amp= \begin{vmatrix} 4 - \lambda \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 - \lambda \end{vmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= (4 - \lambda)(-1 - \lambda) - 2 \cdot 3</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= -4 - 3\lambda + \lambda^2 - 6</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \lambda^2 - 3\lambda - 10.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
      <p>
        In step one, we found that <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = \lambda^2 - 3\lambda - 10</m>, a quadratic in <m>\lambda</m>.
        Now we solve <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Step 2: Solving <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0</m> </title>
      <p>
        Setting the quadratic equal to <m>0</m> and factoring:
        <me>\lambda^2 - 3\lambda - 10 = (\lambda - 5)(\lambda + 2) = 0.</me>
        So the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda = 5</m> and <m>\lambda = -2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    Notice that <m>\lambda = -2</m> is exactly the eigenvalue we confirmed in
    <xref ref="exploration-confirm-eigenvalue"/>; this time we found it from scratch, along
    with a second eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 5</m> that we didn't know about before. Also
    notice that <m>\det(A - \lambda I)</m> came out as a quadratic in
    <m>\lambda</m>. That will always happen for a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix, so we can
    expect (at most) two eigenvalues from this process.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FROM EIGENVALUES TO EIGENVECTORS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now, how do we find the eigenvector that goes with each eigenvalue? We return to the
    second form of the definition:
    <me>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0},</me>
    but this time <m>\lambda</m> is a known number, and the unknown is the vector
    <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Plugging in the
    eigenvalue turns this into a system of equations for <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> that we
    can solve. Let's do <m>\lambda = 5</m> together.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="ex-eigenvector-lambda5">
    <title>Finding an Eigenvector for <m>\lambda = 5</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find an eigenvector of
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
        associated with the eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 5</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We will break this up into two steps. Step 1 will come from solving <m>(A - 5I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>. Step 2
        involves solving for a specific eigenvector.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Step 1: Solving <m>(A - 5I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m> </title>
      <p>
        We solve <m>(A - 5I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>. Subtracting <m>5</m> along the
        diagonal:
        <me>\begin{bmatrix} 4 - 5 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 - 5 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -6 \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        Which means we need to solve the matrix-vector equation:
        <me>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -6 \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Converting to a system of equations (from each row of the equation above):
        <md>
          <mrow>-v_1 + 2v_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
          <mrow>3v_1 - 6v_2 \amp= 0.</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice that the second equation is just <m>-3</m> times the first! So, both
        equations are equivalent, and so the solutions can be described just by the first equation:
        <me>-v_1 + 2v_2 = 0.</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
      <p>
        In step one, we discovered that eigenvectors for <m>A</m> satisfy the relationship
        <me>-v_1 + 2v_2 = 0.</me>
        Now we will find a specific eigenvector.
      </p>
      <solution> <title>Step 2: Finding a specific eigenvector </title>
      <p>
        We can rewrite <m>-v_1 + 2v_2 = 0</m> as
        <me>2v_2 = v_1.</me>
        Notice that there are infinitely many solutions to this. We can fix either component and solve for the other.
        Let's set <m>v_1 = 1</m>. Then
        <m>2v_2 = v_1 = 1</m> gives <m>v_2 = \frac{1}{2}</m>, and:
        <me>\lambda = 5 \implies \mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1/2 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
        Notice, any non-zero multiple also gives a correct answer, for example:
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -1/2 \end{bmatrix}</m> all work. (In fact,
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is arguably the nicest, since it avoids
        fractions.)
      </p>
    </solution>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: TWO THINGS TO NOTICE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-eigenvector-process">
    <title>Two Things to Notice From the Example</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The equations will be redundant.</alert> When <m>\lambda</m> really is an
          eigenvalue, the two equations in the system will always be equivalent (this follows from
          theory in linear algebra we won't go into). So, double check they are in fact multiples of each other, and then
          choose just one equation to find an eigenvector.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>You get to make a choice of fixing either <m>v_1</m> or <m>v_2</m>.</alert> In the previous example, we chose
          <m>v_1 = 1</m>, which determined a specific value for <m>v_2</m>. We could've chosen any other non-zero
          number for <m>v_1</m>. Alternatively, we could've fixed <m>v_2</m> first, instead.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: EIGENVECTOR FOR LAMBDA = -2 (MULTI-PART)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now you can try finding an eigenvector associated with the other eigenvalue, <m>\lambda = -2</m>! The following
    activity walks through the same process, one step at a time.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="act-eigenvector-lambda-neg2">
    <title>Finding an Eigenvector for <m>\lambda = -2</m></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We will find an eigenvector of
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
        associated with the eigenvalue <m>\lambda = -2</m>, by solving
        <m>(A - (-2) I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-neg2-matrix">
      <title>Compute <m>A - (-2)I</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the matrix <m>A - (-2)I</m>? (Careful: subtracting <m>-2</m> means
          <em>adding</em> <m>2</m>!)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A - (-2)I = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Subtracting <m>-2</m> along the diagonal adds <m>2</m> to the
              diagonal entries: <m>4 - (-2) = 6</m> and <m>-1 - (-2) = 1</m>. The
              off-diagonal entries are untouched.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A - (-2)I = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Watch the double negative! This matrix comes from subtracting <m>+2</m> along
              the diagonal, but here <m>\lambda = -2</m>, so the diagonal entries are
              <m>4 - (-2) = 4 + 2 = 6</m> and <m>-1 - (-2) = -1 + 2 = 1</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A - (-2)I = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \amp 4 \\ 5 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Careful: <m>\lambda I = \begin{bmatrix} \lambda \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp \lambda \end{bmatrix}</m>
              only has entries on the <em>diagonal</em>, so only the diagonal entries of
              <m>A</m> change. The off-diagonal entries <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> should stay
              the same.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-neg2-system">
      <title>Convert to a System of Equations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          When solving
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 6 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          what does the system of equations become?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>6v_1 + 2v_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
                <mrow>3v_1 + v_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Each <em>row</em> of the matrix gives one equation: the top row
              gives <m>6v_1 + 2v_2 = 0</m>, and the bottom row gives <m>3v_1 + v_2 = 0</m>.
              As expected, the equations are redundant: the first is just <m>2</m> times
              the second.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>6v_1 + 3v_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
                <mrow>2v_1 + v_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Close, but this uses the <em>columns</em> of the matrix. Each equation comes
              from a <em>row</em>: multiply across the row and down the vector, so the top
              row gives <m>6v_1 + 2v_2 = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>6v_1 + 2v_2 \amp= v_1</mrow>
                <mrow>3v_1 + v_2 \amp= v_2</mrow>
              </md>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Careful with the right-hand side: we are solving
              <m>(A - (-2)I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>, and the <m>\lambda\mathbf{v}</m>
              part has already been moved to the left side inside <m>A - (-2)I</m>. So the
              right-hand side of both equations is just <m>0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-neg2-eigenvector">
      <title>Find a Specific Eigenvector</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Since the two equations are redundant, we can just use the <em>first</em>
          equation: <m>6v_1 + 2v_2 = 0</m>. This time (unlike the example, where we fixed
          <m>v_1</m>) let's fix the <em>second</em> component. Choosing <m>v_2 = 3</m>,
          what is the resulting eigenvector?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! With <m>v_2 = 3</m>, the equation becomes <m>6v_1 + 6 = 0</m>, so
              <m>v_1 = -1</m>. Notice this is <m>-1</m> times the eigenvector
              <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m> we confirmed back on the
              previous page. Both are perfectly valid eigenvectors, since any non-zero
              multiple of an eigenvector is also an eigenvector!
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Close, but watch the sign when solving. With <m>v_2 = 3</m>, the equation is
              <m>6v_1 + 2 \cdot 3 = 0</m>, so <m>6v_1 = -6</m> and <m>v_1 = -1</m>, not
              <m>+1</m>. You can also check directly:
              <m>A\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
              which is not a multiple of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              It looks like the components got swapped! We fixed the <em>second</em>
              component <m>v_2 = 3</m>, and solving <m>6v_1 + 6 = 0</m> gives the
              <em>first</em> component <m>v_1 = -1</m>. Checking directly:
              <m>A\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 10 \\ 10 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
              which is not a multiple of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>
    Putting it all together, we now know everything eigen-related about this matrix:
    <me>\lambda_1 = -2 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix},
      \qquad
      \lambda_2 = 5 \text{ with } \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.</me>
    (Here we chose <m>\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m> rather than the
    <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> from the activity; since one is a multiple of the other, either
    is a fine choice.) Hold onto this matrix and these answers; they are about to make a reappearance when we
    return to differential equations in the next section.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Solve for eigenvalues first:</alert> solve <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = 0</m>. For a
          <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix this is a quadratic in <m>\lambda</m>, so expect two
          eigenvalues.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Then find the eigenvectors:</alert> for each eigenvalue, plug it into
          <m>(A - \lambda I)\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m>, convert to a system of equations,
          and solve for the ratio between <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The two equations when finding eigenvectors are redundant:</alert> the two equations in the system will
          always be multiples of each other when <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue. So, you can just choose one of the equations (coming from the rows)
           to solve for an eigenvector.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Fix a convenient value for either <m>v_1</m> or <m>v_2</m>:</alert> choose any non-zero value for one of the components, such as <m>v_1 = 1</m>,
           which will determine the other component. This will give you one specific eigenvector (there are infinitely many).
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

